    <div class="insight">The global <strong>median homicide rate fell from 3.35 (1990) to 2.27 per 100,000 (2022)</strong>, a reduction of approximately 32%. However, the global mean — heavily influenced by high-rate countries in Latin America and the Caribbean — has remained persistently elevated, declining only modestly from ~8.3 in the 1990s to ~7.7 in the 2010s.</div>

    <p>The 2000s–2010s transition saw the sharpest improvement: the global average fell <strong>9.6%</strong> between these decades. The 2010s–2020s transition showed near-stagnation (<strong>+0.2%</strong>), suggesting the pace of improvement has slowed considerably. Decade averages: 1990s = ''' + str(d['avg_90s']) + ''', 2000s = ''' + str(d['avg_00s']) + ''', 2010s = ''' + str(d['avg_10s']) + ''', 2020s = ''' + str(d['avg_20s']) + '''.</p>

    <p>Reporting coverage expanded substantially from 94 countries in 1990 to a peak of 162 in 2008, then declined to 84 by 2023 — a reporting gap effect that analysts must account for when interpreting recent trends.</p>
    <div class="footnote">Note: Declining coverage in recent years (especially 2022–2023) means recent global statistics are based on a smaller and potentially non-representative sample of countries.</div>
  </div>
</div>

