    <div style="margin-top:20px;">
      <div class="insight"><strong>El Salvador's Transformation:</strong> The most dramatic improvement in the dataset. After peaking at over 100 homicides per 100,000 in the mid-1990s and again around 2015, El Salvador achieved a rate of 7.9 by 2022 — a 94% reduction from its 1994 level of 134.3. This reflects a combination of criminal justice reforms, territorial control policies, and broader security strategies implemented from 2016 onward.</div>

      <div class="insight"><strong>Colombia's Long Arc:</strong> Starting at 75.0 per 100,000 in 1990 — among the world's highest — Colombia reduced its rate by 66.8% to 24.9 by 2023, reflecting decades of security interventions, peace negotiations, and institutional investment.</div>

      <div class="warning"><strong>Caribbean Crisis:</strong> Multiple small Caribbean nations (Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, Trinidad and Tobago, Saint Lucia) have seen dramatic rate increases, often exceeding 300–580% growth over the observation period. These trends reflect the expansion of drug trafficking routes and gang activity. The small population denominator amplifies volatility — a handful of additional homicides can shift rates by multiple points.</div>

