<!-- Section 6: Limitations & Recommendations -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">06</span>
    <h2>Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="section-body">
    <h3 style="font-size:14px; font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin-bottom:12px;">Data Limitations</h3>
    <div class="limitations-grid">
      <div class="limit-card">
        <h4>Unbalanced Panel Dataset</h4>
        <p>The dataset is an unbalanced panel: not all 201 countries report in every year. The reporting set expands from 94 countries (1990) to a peak of 162 (2008), then contracts to 84 (2023). Global averages computed across different subsets of countries are not directly comparable across time.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-card">
        <h4>No Missing Value Imputation</h4>
        <p>Rows for country-year combinations without available data are simply absent — no interpolation was applied. This is methodologically sound but means analysts should not assume continuity where gaps exist.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-card">
        <h4>United Kingdom Caveat</h4>
        <p>UK data in this dataset refer to <strong>England and Wales only</strong>, as reported in the UNODC source. Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries. Aggregating these to a "United Kingdom" total requires external population weights.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-card">
        <h4>Territory Double-Counting Risk</h4>
        <p>Dependent territories (Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico, British Overseas Territories) are reported separately and should not be aggregated with their parent countries. Care is required when constructing national or regional totals.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-card">
        <h4>Small Population Volatility</h4>
        <p>Small island states and territories with populations under 100,000 can exhibit extreme rate fluctuations driven by a small number of absolute homicides. Rates above 50 per 100,000 in these jurisdictions should be interpreted with caution.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit-card">
        <h4>Sex/Age Coverage Gaps</h4>
        <p>Sex-disaggregated and age-disaggregated data have substantially lower coverage than aggregate (sex=both, age=ALL) records. Gender and age analyses in this report are limited to the reporting subset and may not generalize globally.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 style="font-size:14px; font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin: 24px 0 12px;">Policy Recommendations</h3>
    <ul class="rec-list">
      <li><strong>Prioritize Caribbean and Central American Regional Interventions</strong>
        Latin America and the Caribbean consistently record rates 10–20× higher than Europe and Asia. International cooperation frameworks should prioritize capacity-building, anti-trafficking enforcement, and social investment in these regions, particularly in small island states experiencing rapid rate escalation (Ecuador, Haiti, Trinidad and Tobago).</li>
      <li><strong>Targeted Youth Violence Prevention (Ages 18–29)</strong>
        The 18–29 age cohort faces the highest homicide risk globally. Evidence-based interventions — including cognitive behavioral therapy programs, economic opportunity initiatives, and hotspot policing — should be scaled in high-rate jurisdictions, informed by the strong age-risk gradient identified in this data.</li>
      <li><strong>Gender-Responsive Homicide Policy</strong>
        The persistent male-to-female ratio of 2.6× masks distinct underlying drivers. While male homicide is concentrated in public spaces and linked to organized crime, female homicide frequently involves intimate partner and domestic contexts. Separate policy streams addressing femicide prevention, shelter infrastructure, and legal accountability are warranted alongside general homicide reduction strategies.</li>
      <li><strong>Invest in Reporting Capacity to Close Data Gaps</strong>
        With only 53% of countries providing data in 2022 and coverage declining since 2008, the global picture is increasingly incomplete. UNODC and member states should invest in national civil registration systems and standardized homicide reporting protocols to restore and expand coverage — particularly in sub-Saharan Africa and conflict-affected states.</li>
      <li><strong>Monitor and Study Successful Outliers</strong>
        Countries like El Salvador, Colombia, and the Philippines achieved dramatic reductions exceeding 65% over the observation period. Systematic evaluation of the policy, institutional, and social factors driving these reductions would generate transferable lessons for other high-burden contexts.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

